{"records":[{"id":"bv253dc4rb","title":"Manuscrito scAAV2 genome","description":"Data used for Figures on the Manuscript Real-time Genome Imaging of Host Interactions in Adeno-Associated Virus Genome Release","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-02T09:46:31.12Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bv253dc4rb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bv253dc4rb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Luisa","last_name":"Bustamante Jaramillo","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01tm6cn81","name":"University of Gothenburg","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Gene Therapy","Host Factor","Viral Entry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hwzfrkxhzh","title":"Transduction of Murine Alveolar Macrophages with Adeno-associated Virus 6: Implications for Cell and Gene Therapy","description":"Raw Data for the manuscript \"Transduction of Murine Alveolar Macrophages with Adeno-associated Virus 6: Implications for Cell and Gene Therapy\".\nAbstract:\nAlveolar macrophages (AMs) are self-renewing, tissue resident cells of the lung. Their genetic manipulation remains challenging but due to their longevity they are promising tools for gene or cell therapy. \nMurine AMs were isolated from C57BL/6J mice and characterized for purity and gene expression profile. AMs were cultured and transduced with different AAV serotypes expressing green fluorescent protein (GFP) to investigate the effects of serotype, multiplicity of infection (MOI), and incubation time, and analyzed by live cell and confocal microscopy. Transducing AMs with AAV6 at a MOI of 87,500 resulted in the highest transduction rate, transgene signal intensity, and expression after 6 days. Activation of AM by lipopolysaccharide (LPS) stimulation significantly increased GFP protein synthesis. AAV transduction did not induce inflammation in AMs and transgene signal intensity was stable after the removal of the LPS stimulus. In conclusion, murine AMs can be successfully cultured in vitro, AAV6 showed the highest transduction efficiency, and activation by LPS significantly increases transgene expression. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-15T17:12:05.128Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hwzfrkxhzh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hwzfrkxhzh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Isabell","last_name":"Theisohn","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Heiko","last_name":"Heilmann","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lukas","last_name":"Busch","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bernd","last_name":"Bufe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Felix","last_name":"Ritzmann","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christoph","last_name":"Beisswenger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Bals","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Herr","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03v7rwj71","name":"Dr. Rolf M. Schwiete Stiftung","grant_id":"MACROBASE"}],"categories":["Cell Therapy","Alveolar Macrophage","Personalized Therapy","Adeno-Associated Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hvbvrtncbp","title":"Tamura et al Supplementary Fig. 1S","description":"This is the information of adeno-associated virus (Supplementary Fig. 1S by Tamura et al. )","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-07T07:15:52.924Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hvbvrtncbp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hvbvrtncbp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kenjiro","last_name":"Seki","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biological Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pwmk63vf2s","title":"Dataset for Quantum cascade laser-based mid-infrared spectroscopy (QCL-MIDIR) for real-time monitoring of Adeno-associated virus particles  ","description":"This study demonstrates the application of Quantum cascade laser-based mid-infrared spectroscopy (QCL-MIDIR) combined with Partial Least Squares Discriminant Analysis for real-time monitoring of Adeno-associated virus particles without sample preparation. This dataset comprises raw and processed data, along with the Python scripts associated with the manuscript. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-13T17:50:01.929Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pwmk63vf2s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pwmk63vf2s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shreya Milind","last_name":"Athalye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Neelesh","last_name":"Sarathy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juancarlos","last_name":"Rosario","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mohit","last_name":"Verma","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01qat3289","name":"Eli Lilly (United States)","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Spectroscopy","Laser-Based Technique","Process Analytical Technology","Adeno-Associated Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m6j6jzzt36","title":"Oxygen-dependent histone lysine demethylase 4 restricts hepatitis B virus replication","description":"Mammalian cells have evolved strategies to regulate gene expression when oxygen is limited. Hypoxia inducible factors (HIF) are the major transcriptional regulators of host gene expression. We previously reported that HIFs bind and activate hepatitis B virus (HBV) DNA transcription under low oxygen conditions; however, the global cellular response to low oxygen is mediated by a family of oxygenases that work in concert with HIFs. Recent studies have identified a role for chromatin modifiers in sensing cellular oxygen and orchestrating transcriptional responses, but their role in the HBV life cycle is as yet undefined. We demonstrated that histone lysine demethylase 4 (KDM4) can restrict HBV, and pharmacological or oxygen-mediated inhibition of the demethylase increases viral RNAs derived from both episomal and integrated copies of the viral genome. Sequencing studies demonstrated that KDM4 is a major regulator of the hepatic transcriptome, which defines hepatocellular permissivity to HBV infection. We propose a model where HBV exploits cellular oxygen sensors to replicate and persist in the liver. Understanding oxygen-dependent pathways that regulate HBV infection will facilitate the development of physiologically relevant cell-based models that support efficient HBV replication.  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-22T12:25:21.16Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m6j6jzzt36","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m6j6jzzt36"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"James","last_name":"Harris","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing","Chromatin Immunoprecipitation","Western Blot","Real-Time Polymerase Chain Reaction","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tkkh3styfy","title":"Summary of ocular adeno-associated virus (AAV) gene therapy studies","description":"Gene therapy using adeno-associated virus (AAV) as a vector represents a novel therapeutic modality to treat ocular diseases by transferring engineered genetic material into target cells for the purpose of preventing, halting or reversing pathological processes.\n\nDespite the enormous advantages of AAV-based gene therapy, there are still concerns about long-term efficacy and safety issues. Given the rising of this groundbreaking approach, we summarized a dataset of ocular AAV gene therapy studies (trials/publications).\n\nData source: ClinicalTrials.gov, Embase and PubMed.\n\nSearch strategy: Strategies are provided in the folder \" Extraction process\".\n\nData dictionary: Unifying each variable, a normalized list of data items is provided in the folder \" Extraction process\".\n\nA trial was eligible for inclusion if the following criteria was met: (1) the study focused on patients with ocular diseases who received rAAV-based gene therapy; for publications, additional criteria were (2) study eyes for treatment are unilateral; (3) there was a clearly disclosed drug information and route of administration (ROA); and (4) the article reported the safety outcomes following gene therapy. If results from one trial were reported in several publications, data included in the ‘Dataset of ocular AAV gene therapy clinical studies.xlsx’ were extracted from the publication that was considered the most appropriate to avoid using duplicate data. If a trial consisted of several phases and was reported separately in the publication, we included the publication from different phases separately. For trial with multiple publications, we list our reason for selection in the folder \" Extraction process\".\n\nFor each study, extracted data included: (1) study characteristics (condition, age, sex, start date, region, phase, status, funder type, study design and enrollment); (2) interventions (study eye, dose, ROA, drug information, and prophylactic immunosuppression regimen); and (3) details of adverse event (AE) and/or a specific safety outcome and/or immune response.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-11T21:12:50.901Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tkkh3styfy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tkkh3styfy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zishi","last_name":"Liu","orcid":"0009-0008-0650-5241"},{"first_name":"Tong","last_name":"Li","orcid":"0000-0002-2544-1254"},{"first_name":"Huixun","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03kt66j61","name":"Science and Technology Commission of Shanghai Municipality","grant_id":"21ZR1451500"},{"id":"https://ror.org/027s68j25","name":"Ministry of Science and Technology of the People's Republic of China","grant_id":"2022YFC2502800"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"U22A20311"},{"id":"https://ror.org/03kt66j61","name":"Science and Technology Commission of Shanghai Municipality","grant_id":"20Z11900400"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82101168"}],"categories":["Clinical Trial","Gene Therapy","Eye","Retinal Disease","Clinical Trial Design","Clinical Trial Results","Adeno-Associated Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3h325jhj93","title":"Detection of a complex chromosomal rearrangement in a novel mouse mutant by optical genome mapping","description":"The purpose of this study was to identify the genomic rearrangements present in a transgenic mouse line (Line 781) that expresses a lacZ reporter gene cassette. Mice hemizygous for the transgene have a mutant phenotype (smaller body size and paws, and craniofacial aberrations), whereas their non-transgenic littermates are phenotypically normal. The transgene inserted near or at a reciprocal translocation (RT) between chromosomes 1 and 2 as determined by fluorescent in situ hybridization (FISH) analysis. Optical genome mapping (OGM) was performed to better identify the genomic rearrangements present in the mutant mouse genome. OGM was performed with ultra-high molecular weight genomic DNA isolated from a mutant female mouse, with an average length of 246.092 kbp. The DNA was labeled with DL-green fluorophores at CTTAAG sites with DLE-1 methyltransferase. Because the target site is palindromic, both DNA strands get labeled. The label density averaged 15.017 labels per 100 kb. The labeled DNA was analyzed on the Bionano Saphyr system to measure the distance between labels (tags) for each single, extremely long strand of genomic DNA (‘molecule’). The resulting data was entered into the de novo assembly pipeline to group the molecules by label pattern similarity in order to create maps, which then were used to construct the mutant genome. The rare variant analysis pipeline was used to compare the assembly against a reference genome (GRCm38.p6). Areas of discrepancy within the mutant genome were identified using the structural variant (SV) caller. It indicated that the RT junction in der2 was separated by extraneous DNA, which happens to be the transgene. Based on target sites for labeling within the transgene sequence, it was estimated that five complete copies of the transgene integrated in a tail-to-head orientation, directly between the breakpoints in der2. The breakpoints in der2 were estimated to be in the vicinity of positions chr2:106,476,853 and chr1:163,466,814. OGM results also estimated the RT junction in der1 occurs near positions chr1:159,566,643 and chr2:108,255,381. Taken together, the data suggest that parts of chromosomes 1 and 2 were deleted during the reorganization of the mutant genome. As well, copy number variant (CNV) analysis was performed for the mutant genome using the Bionano software. The analysis confirmed areas of monosomy within chromosomes 1 and 2, which correspond to gaps of missing sequence in the derivative chromosomes when the SV caller estimated breakpoints are considered in combination. Thus, the RT is unbalanced, with segments of chr1 (3.9 Mbp) and chr2 (1.8 Mbp) missing from the derivative chromosomes. The breakpoints were identified precisely by Sanger sequencing of PCR products that span a junction between chromosomal breakpoints or with the transgene. Thus, it is likely that the phenotype in mutant animals is due to the deletion of a gene or genes within the deleted sequence.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-30T18:52:53.931Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3h325jhj93","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3h325jhj93"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Gretchen","last_name":"Holtgrefe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patricia","last_name":"Wight","orcid":"0000-0003-3841-6143"}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"UAMS Barton Pilot Study","grant_id":null},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01 NS106179-01"}],"categories":["Genetics","Chromosomal Aberration","Transgenic Mouse","Chromosomal Translocation","Optical Genome Mapping"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7nsphs4fjm","title":"Transgenic rescue results with a null mutant of Drosophila Nkt","description":"Figure. A genomic Nkt+ transgene rescues sleep in a null mutant whereas a UAS transgene expressing in only glia or neurons does not. A) Rescue of sleep in an Nkt null mutant (L2-11) by one copy of a fosmid transgene expressing NKT+-sGFP under control of the endogenous promoter. B-C) Lack of rescue of the null L2-11 mutant with glial (B) or neuronal (C) expression of a UAS-Nkt+ transgene. Sleep amount in null mutants expressing Nkt+ was not significantly different from that of mutant controls. The D. virilis U-Dv_Nkt+ transgene – which can rescue sleep in knockdown flies – was employed in these experiments. ***, p<0.001.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-07-30T14:40:45.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7nsphs4fjm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7nsphs4fjm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rob","last_name":"Jackson","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Animal Sleep"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sj8yykm6bn","title":"Raw data for Sati et al., 2020","description":"These datasets are contain Beta gal assay images, DAPI stained nuclei and 3D FISH images from oncogene and replicative senescent cells. The Betagal assay was performed to demonstrate the senescent condition. The DAPI stained nuclei images are from different siRNA treated OIS samples. The FISH images display the presence of SHADs in SAHFs.\n\nThe Summary of work:\nWhile transcriptional and epigenetic changes associated with senescence are well studied, the role of the extensive senescence-associated 3D genome reorganization remains elusive. Here, we have generated genome wide chromatin interaction maps, epigenetic, replication-timing, whole genome bisulfite sequencing and gene expression profiles from cells entering replicative senescence (RS) or upon oncogene induced senescence (OIS). We identify Senescence Associated Heterochromatin Domains (SAHDs). Differential intra vs inter SAHD interactions lead to the formation of senescence associated heterochromatin foci (SAHFs) in OIS but not in RS. This OIS-specific configuration brings active genes located in genomic regions adjacent to SAHDs in close spatial proximity and favours their expression. Finally, screening of factors for SAHF induction revealed DNMT1 as a novel component that induces SAHFs by promoting HMGA2 expression. Upon DNMT1 depletion, OIS cells transition to a 3D genome conformation akin to that of cells in replicative senescence. These data show how multi-omics and imaging can identify critical features of RS and OIS and discover new determinants of acute senescence and SAHF formation.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-02-13T18:08:00.967Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sj8yykm6bn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sj8yykm6bn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Satish","last_name":"Sati","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Giacomo","last_name":"Cavalli","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chromosome","Chromatin","Cellular Senescence","Genome","Epigenetics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"c35h764nmv","title":"Placental whole genome RNA sequencing dataset of monochorionic diamniotic twin pregnancies with and without selective fetal growth restriction","description":"To compare placental transcriptomes between selective fetal growth restriction (sFGR) twins and normally grown twins (Control) in monochorionic diamniotic (MCDA) twin pregnancies, placentae from five sFGR and three control twin pairs were collected. Their placental transcriptomes were profiled by high throughput total whole genome RNA sequencing technology, which provides a global and deep examination of gene expression. The log2 ratio of the placental transcriptome expression levels of the smaller twin to that of the larger co-twin were calculated in each twin pair. Results in sFGR pregnancies were compared to that of the control pregnancies. Differentially expressed transcripts identified as the within-pair log expression ratio difference between sFGR and control groups were determined by Mann-Whitney U test. Based on these differentially expressed transcripts, biological pathways and upstream regulators were predicted using the Ingenuity Pathway Analysis (IPA) software. This dataset describes total 1429 differentially expressed placental transcripts, total 126 predicted biological pathways and 419 upstream regulators in sFGR. The data provide further insight in transcriptomic level to the pathomechansim of pregnancies complicated by sFGR.\nThe dataset supports the research article entitled “Monochorionic twins with selective fetal growth restriction: insight from placental whole transcriptome analysis”.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-11T11:28:13.791Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/c35h764nmv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/c35h764nmv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Wei ","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Claire Yik Lok ","last_name":"Chung","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chi Chiu ","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ting Fung","last_name":" Chan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maran Bo Wah ","last_name":"Leung","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Oi Ka ","last_name":"Chan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ling ","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kubi ","last_name":"Appiah","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Piya ","last_name":"Chaemsaithong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yvonne Kwun Yue ","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liona Chiu Yee ","last_name":"Poon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tak Yeung ","last_name":"Leung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Human"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":130435,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":171,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":130435,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":129859,"restricted":69,"embargoed":507},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2160},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1369},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":620},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":462},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":436},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":407},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":398},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":370},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":315},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":255}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":127820},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1896},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":179},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":132},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466